// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"AP Art History covers 250 works across 10 content areas. We begin with humanity's earliest art.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Prehistoric art (before about 3000 BCE) includes cave paintings (Lascaux, Chauvet), small portable figures, and monumental structures like Stonehenge, likely tied to ritual and astronomy."},{"t":"key","text":"In the Ancient Near East, art legitimized rulers and honored gods: the White Temple and ziggurat of Uruk, the Code of Hammurabi stele (showing the king receiving law from a god), and Assyrian lamassu guarding palace gates."},{"t":"p","text":"Egyptian art emphasized permanence and the afterlife: the Great Pyramids, the Palette of Narmer (hierarchical scale, with the king shown largest), and the Amarna period's unusual naturalism under Akhenaten."}]},{"say":"Greek and Roman art shaped Western ideals of beauty, proportion and power for centuries.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Greek art evolved from the stiff Archaic kouros, to the Classical period's idealized balance (Polykleitos's Doryphoros, using contrapposto and a canon of proportions), to the Hellenistic period's drama and emotion (the Laocoön, the Winged Victory of Samothrace)."},{"t":"p","text":"The Parthenon (Athens) used refined proportions and the Doric order; its sculptures celebrated Athena and Athenian identity."},{"t":"p","text":"The Etruscans made terra-cotta sculptures and tombs that influenced Rome."},{"t":"p","text":"Roman art served the empire: realistic portraits (veristic busts), propaganda like the Augustus of Prima Porta, and engineering: the Colosseum and the Pantheon's concrete dome with its oculus."}]}],[{"say":"From glowing mosaics to Renaissance masterpieces, European art reflected changing religious and cultural ideas.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Early Christian and Byzantine art (Hagia Sophia, the Justinian mosaics at San Vitale) used gold backgrounds and frontal figures to express the spiritual and the emperor's divine authority."},{"t":"p","text":"Romanesque and Gothic churches (Chartres Cathedral) used architecture and stained glass to teach and inspire pilgrims."},{"t":"p","text":"The Renaissance revived classical forms and humanism: Donatello's David (first freestanding nude since antiquity), Brunelleschi's dome, Leonardo's Last Supper, Michelangelo's Sistine Chapel and Raphael's School of Athens. In the North, Jan van Eyck's Arnolfini Portrait used oil paint for detail and symbolism."}]},{"say":"The Baroque brought drama and emotion, and colonization blended European and Indigenous traditions.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Baroque used drama, movement and light to inspire faith (the Counter-Reformation) and display power: Bernini's Ecstasy of Saint Teresa, Caravaggio's Calling of Saint Matthew, Velázquez's Las Meninas, Rembrandt's Night Watch, and the Palace of Versailles."},{"t":"p","text":"In the colonial Americas, art combined European styles with Indigenous materials, techniques and beliefs. Casta paintings depicted the racial hierarchy of New Spain. The Virgin of Guadalupe and churches throughout Latin America show this blending."},{"t":"p","text":"Dutch Golden Age art, supported by a merchant class, featured everyday scenes, portraits and still lifes (Vermeer, Ruysch)."}]}],[{"say":"Between 1750 and 1900, art responded to revolution, industry and photography.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Neoclassicism (David's Oath of the Horatii) promoted civic virtue. Romanticism (Goya's Third of May, 1808; Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People) expressed emotion and political passion. Realism (Courbet's Stone Breakers) depicted ordinary working people."},{"t":"p","text":"Photography challenged painting's role in recording reality. Impressionists (Monet's Saint-Lazare Station, Morisot) captured light and modern life; Post-Impressionists (Van Gogh, Cézanne, Gauguin, Seurat) explored structure, color and emotion."},{"t":"p","text":"The Industrial Revolution brought new materials like iron and glass (the Crystal Palace)."}]},{"say":"In the 20th century, artists broke every rule, exploring abstraction, the unconscious and mass culture.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Picasso's Les Demoiselles d'Avignon launched Cubism, fragmenting form and drawing on African and Iberian art. Other movements: Futurism (speed and technology), Dada (anti-art, Duchamp's Fountain), Surrealism (Dalí, Magritte, Kahlo), and the Bauhaus (functional design)."},{"t":"p","text":"After World War II, Abstract Expressionism (Pollock, Rothko) made New York the art world's center. Pop Art (Warhol's Marilyn Diptych) used mass media imagery. Architecture: Frank Lloyd Wright's Fallingwater, Le Corbusier's Villa Savoye."},{"t":"tip","text":"For modern works, connect the style to its moment: war, technology and new ideas about the mind all pushed artists away from realistic representation."}]}],[{"say":"Art from Africa and the Indigenous Americas often served spiritual, political and social roles within communities.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Indigenous Americas: Chavín de Huántar and Machu Picchu (Andes), the Templo Mayor and Coatlicue sculpture (Aztec), Mesa Verde cliff dwellings (Ancestral Puebloans), and Northwest Coast transformation masks. Many works connect to ritual, cosmology and power."},{"t":"p","text":"African art: many works were made to be used in performances, rituals and leadership, not just viewed. Examples include the Great Mosque of Djenné (Mali, earthen architecture), Benin bronze plaques, Kuba and Yoruba masks, and power figures (nkisi n'kondi) used for oaths and healing."},{"t":"p","text":"Art should be understood in its original context: a mask comes alive in performance with music and dance."}]},{"say":"Asian and Pacific art spans thousands of years and connects deeply to religion, nature and society.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"West and Central Asia: the Dome of the Rock and the Great Mosque of Isfahan (Islamic architecture with calligraphy and geometric design), the Kaaba (the focus of Islamic pilgrimage)."},{"t":"p","text":"South, East and Southeast Asia: the Great Stupa at Sanchi (Buddhist), Angkor Wat (Hindu then Buddhist temple in Cambodia), the Taj Mahal (Mughal tomb), the Forbidden City (China), Japanese woodblock prints like Hokusai's Great Wave off Kanagawa (which influenced Impressionists), and Chinese scroll paintings."},{"t":"p","text":"The Pacific: Moai on Easter Island (Rapa Nui), Hawaiian feather cloaks ('ahu 'ula) showing chiefly status, and navigation charts from the Marshall Islands."}]}],[{"say":"Since 1980, art has become global, addressing identity, politics and technology in new forms.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Contemporary art uses many media: installation, performance, video, digital and land art. It often addresses identity (race, gender, culture), globalization, memory and the environment."},{"t":"p","text":"Examples include Maya Lin's Vietnam Veterans Memorial (a reflective black granite wall listing names), Yayoi Kusama's immersive installations, El Anatsui's shimmering bottle-cap tapestries, and Kara Walker's silhouettes confronting racism."},{"t":"p","text":"Many contemporary artists question who gets represented in art and museums, and challenge the traditional Western canon."}]},{"say":"The AP Art History exam asks you to analyze works using form, function, content and context. Here's how.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Analyze works using four lenses:"},{"t":"list","items":["Form: materials, techniques, composition and style.","Function: the purpose (religious, political, commemorative).","Content: subject matter and iconography.","Context: when, where, by whom and for whom it was made."]},{"t":"p","text":"Free-response types include comparison, visual and contextual analysis, attribution (identifying an unknown work's culture or artist by its features), and continuity and change."},{"t":"p","text":"Always use specific visual evidence and connect it to meaning. Learn identifying details of the 250 required works: title, artist or culture, date and material."}]}]];
export default text;
